{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/deviant-the-renegades/1542465-ruminations-about-my-next-campaign","id":1542465,"title":"Ruminations about my next campaign","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Deviant: The Renegades"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-09-20T12:07:40-07:00","id":1542465,"title":"Ruminations about my next campaign","content":"I\u2019m a professional Game Master specializing in campaigns that center Leather, BDSM, and kink culture. The word \u201cculture\u201d is important there; while I am open to and intrigued by the narrative possibilities of erotic play, my focus is on exploring, incorporating, and (by sheer nature of most TTRPG experiences) mythologizing the lifeways, values, history, and relationships of my community ~ the history of a leather vest or the politics surrounding running a dungeon or the psychological development of a Leather person over their life or the romance of a D/s relationship are more important to me than extraneous depictions of any given scene or hookup.<br /> <br /> One of my former players, who had joined my kink culture-centering Mage: the Awakening game <i>Sophia in Chains: The Safeword is \u201cParadox\u201d</i>, now wants me to run a game of Deviant: the Renegades in my typical mode. I\u2019ven\u2019t actually looked at Deviant before (just now read all three books of the gameline), so unlike Sophia in Chains, I don\u2019t already have a grasp of its themes and narrative potentials that could be readily adapted to such a campaign.<br /> <br /> This thread is intended to be a place where I can ruminate on possibilities for themes, narrative beats or direction, and other such GM-y things, and maybe get some commentary or ideas from the crowd. I have a document where I pulled bits and pieces of text that seemed evocative and noted the ideas t inspired next to them, and that\u2019s what I\u2019m working from for the most part in this thread.<br /> <br /> One of the first <b>themes</b> that occurs to me is perhaps the most obvious, though perhaps one of the less likely to be included in play. Deviants, perhaps just as much as changelings, are creatures born of trauma. I mean, their souls are literally broken; that\u2019s the whole deal of how Divergence works! Some people who engage in kink, BDSM, Leather, WIITWD, or any of the various interwoven cultures involved in my campaigns do so as a means of processing trauma. There are a wide variety of ways in which any given activity or relationship can do so: simple catharsis, reclaiming the ability to consent, receiving aftercare where one had not before, etc. It\u2019s an inherently risky practice, of course. While there are, perhaps unsurprisingly, quite a few therapists in these spaces, most of us aren\u2019t.<br /> <br /> More humbly, this <b>theme</b> could possibly be construed simply as an examination of aftercare, magnifying it to an ongoing story theme to explore how it works, how to do it better, what it means, and its failures and inevitable surprising connections.<br /> <br /> The theme given in the Deviants corebook ~ Isolation ~ might not immediately seem to connect with the kinds of things about which I wanna tell collaborative stories. However, the focus described in its breakdown is on difficulty connecting, severance from the \u201cshared bond of the human soul that brings human beings together.\u201d While this might be an exaggeration of the reality of most of us, it is true that I have occasionally felt some distance yawning between me and more vanilla people in my life. Some of that, of course, is simply due to the myriad factors pushing us, both rightly and wrongly, to euphemize and mask how our lives and relationships work, referring to one\u2019s Dominant as one\u2019s spouse, for example. Another piece playing into it for some is the awkwardness that comes from visible marks of one\u2019s identity: collars, bruises, and protocols. These must be hidden or explained in some way that won\u2019t invite discomfort or invasive probing. But some of it also comes from something that feels more like what Deviant is talking about. There can be a troubling alienation that comes from one\u2019s love and pleasure differing greatly from what is described by the overculture as \u201cgood\u201d or \u201cright\u201d. This can include our communities providing the space for ace and aro people to explore how connection works for them, but also comes from seeming challenge to ideas like love avoiding causing pain, love being egalitarian, shared pleasure, etc. Maybe I could recast the <b>theme</b> of Isolation from the gameline to discuss this, again probably exaggerated but still present, reality . . .<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>either e/em/eir pronouns OR fey/fem/fear OR be/bim/bos pronouns {if you don't want to learn new words, singular they is OK}<br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/queermythopoeia\" target=\"_blank\">Support my RPG writing and streaming on Patreon</a>!<br /> <a href=\"https://luciferssubcreations.blogspot.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Shards: Sub-Creations Taking Root in Ein Soph Aur</a> {an rpg blog}","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26484-tristissima-et-alia","name":"Tristissima et alia","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-23T15:45:38-07:00","id":1542535,"title":"","content":"I can actually see a Deviant game working with such themes. I think the main problem would be that the types of isolation involved would be different. For those with niche interests, the isolation is due to an internal attribute that manifested largely independently of outside circumstances. In contrast, most types of Deviants have the circumstances of the Divergence unwillingly thrust upon them by events in the world. In practice, I think that the isolation would be self-perpetuated in largely the same manner. Both individuals would essentially need to distance themselves from the rest of humanity in order to maintain their safety and livelihood. Deviants, however, could view themselves as victimized in a more unambiguous manner than the other person could. Unless they are Autourgics, they did not really choose to undergo the Divergence. It just happened with them. The same could be said about those with niche interests; after all, a person does not choose their fetishes. However, due to societal influences, I think that such an individual would probably be more likely to feel guilt and shame. A Deviant has a Conspiracy who is rather unambiguously the enemy. The other individual does not, and might be more liable to see the enemy within, in the form of presumed internal flaws and the like. I think that the scenario that you present would actually work in a really interesting way with Autourgics. Funnily enough, I can actually see how you ran a campaign with similar themes using the Mage: The Awakening rules. Mage: The Awakening has a theme of separation from humanity in which the primary difference is internal, like the situation with the individual with niche interests. I remember that the Free Council in 2e had some stuff that referenced fetishism, and so I am guessing that I am not the only person who noticed the parallels. In any case, I wish you well with your campaign.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-23T19:42:16-07:00","id":1542538,"title":"","content":"I think calling out the idea of guilt and shame is a good one, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"28352\" data-vbnamecard=\"28352\">Verlan</a> ~ certainly it is a common element in the histories of kinky folx! It can also, as always, be leveraged by a conspiracy to control a Remade, offering them both the freedom to indulge and the false hope of a way to rid oneself of one's &quot;failings&quot;. I think a truly beautiful story could be told about such a character discovering the Leather community and having the twin experience of &quot;Wait, it's okay for me to like/do this?!?&quot; and of having to learn good consent/aftercare/etc. practices, with the grief, sorrow, and self-recrimination that can come from having to face one's wrongdoings. However, of course, redemption arcs are famously difficult to do without falling into the trap of becoming an excuse arc.<br /> <br /> As I went through the standard Chapter 1, the usual menu of splats from which prospective players can choose, I naturally couldn\u2019t help pondering concepts for Deviants involved in BDSM, kink, Leather, WIITWD, etc.<br /> <br /> The Autourgics (a fancy word from the Greek \u03b1\u1f50\u03c4\u03cc\u03c2 \u201cself\u201d and -\u03bf\u03c5\u03c1\u03b3\u03cc\u03c2 \u201cworker, maker\u201d; in a typical CoD move, they are distinct from the Self-Made despite having essentially the same name) or Elect (a religious Christian term meaning those who are chosen by God, foreordained to Heaven, or have seen the true reality) chose their Divergence. In addition to the obvious trans allegory, body modification can be its own fetish, as can transformation. Anyone might pursue their interest in changing their bodies into becoming Remade. Right alongside transformation fetish is various kinds of archetype play: bimbofication, dronification, Stepford, petplay, etc. Lifestyle practitioners of this kind of interest might easily jump at a chance to shape themselves into the kind of person or being that arouses them. And, of course, some people are simply seekers of new sensations, chasing the high of experiencing something they have never experienced before. Imagine if someone offered one such the ability to gain entirely new senses with which they can explore! You kinda don\u2019t have to, as one of the sample Elect is, in fact, basically this, though with only a light nod to fetish.<br /> <br /> However, the most intriguing ideas to me are twofold. The first involves corruption kink, which is almost a form of archetype play, only the archetype in question is simply \u201cworse\u201d (whatever that means in the role-play or for the individual in question). Parts of the description of the Autourgic in the book almost play into this, even sounding like something that might be said in the context of that kink, such as \u201cLook at what you are now. Look what you wanted.\u201d The other is someone in a Total Power Exchange relationship who is ordered to choose the Divergence. Arguably, that latter would count more as a Epimorph instead.<br /> <br /> The Epimorph (from \u1f10\u03c0\u1fd0\u0301 \u201con, upon, on top of\u201d and \u03bc\u03bf\u03c1\u03c6\u03ae \u201cshape, form, appearance, kind, type\u201d; presumably it was chosen in reference to a healing process wherein specialized cells revert to less specialized cells, which . . . is kinda creepy, to be honest) are those who Volunteered for the Divergence, even if they didn\u2019t pursue it for its own sake. In addition to the idea mentioned at the end of the previous paragraph, I had only a single immediate character come to mind: a Dominant or sadist who might be newer to the scene than their submissive or masochistic partner and thus feels inadequate, not quite able to give their partner what they want and need. In my experience, this feeling is both common and almost always wrong. However, I can fully see someone in the Chronicles of Darkness turning to the Divergence as a way to reshape themselves into what they think a \u201cproper\u201d or \u201ctrue\u201d Dominant or sadist should be. I used the other side of this (a submissive who felt like they weren\u2019t a good enough submissive) in <i>Sophia in Chains: The Safeword is Paradox</i>, actually.<br /> <br /> The Genotypal (we all know what that means, right?) or Born seem to lend themselves to the idea of a second-generation kinkster. Not someone \u201cborn into it\u201d (vomit) or a victim of certain things, but simply someone, like many of my friends, whose parents/parenting community happen to have also been involved in the community and who thus has more ability to contextualize their interests/identity and some readymade protectors when they enter the scene as an adult ~ and who (hopefully) also has some excellent practice drawing healthy boundaries! To be honest, that is the only character concept that suggested itself to me for the Genotypal ~ I suspect that they are the hardest to fit into this campaign concept . . . .","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>either e/em/eir pronouns OR fey/fem/fear OR be/bim/bos pronouns {if you don't want to learn new words, singular they is OK}<br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/queermythopoeia\" target=\"_blank\">Support my RPG writing and streaming on Patreon</a>!<br /> <a href=\"https://luciferssubcreations.blogspot.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Shards: Sub-Creations Taking Root in Ein Soph Aur</a> {an rpg blog}","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26484-tristissima-et-alia","name":"Tristissima et alia","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-09-24T16:37:49-07:00","id":1542593,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tristissima et alia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/deviant-the-renegades/1542465-ruminations-about-my-next-campaign?p=1542538#post1542538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think calling out the idea of guilt and shame is a good one, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"28352\" data-vbnamecard=\"28352\">Verlan</a> ~ certainly it is a common element in the histories of kinky folx! It can also, as always, be leveraged by a conspiracy to control a Remade, offering them both the freedom to indulge and the false hope of a way to rid oneself of one's &quot;failings&quot;. I think a truly beautiful story could be told about such a character discovering the Leather community and having the twin experience of &quot;Wait, it's okay for me to like/do this?!?&quot; and of having to learn good consent/aftercare/etc. practices, with the grief, sorrow, and self-recrimination that can come from having to face one's wrongdoings. However, of course, redemption arcs are famously difficult to do without falling into the trap of becoming an excuse arc.<br /> <br /> As I went through the standard Chapter 1, the usual menu of splats from which prospective players can choose, I naturally couldn\u2019t help pondering concepts for Deviants involved in BDSM, kink, Leather, WIITWD, etc.<br /> <br /> The Autourgics (a fancy word from the Greek \u03b1\u1f50\u03c4\u03cc\u03c2 \u201cself\u201d and -\u03bf\u03c5\u03c1\u03b3\u03cc\u03c2 \u201cworker, maker\u201d; in a typical CoD move, they are distinct from the Self-Made despite having essentially the same name) or Elect (a religious Christian term meaning those who are chosen by God, foreordained to Heaven, or have seen the true reality) chose their Divergence. In addition to the obvious trans allegory, body modification can be its own fetish, as can transformation. Anyone might pursue their interest in changing their bodies into becoming Remade. Right alongside transformation fetish is various kinds of archetype play: bimbofication, dronification, Stepford, petplay, etc. Lifestyle practitioners of this kind of interest might easily jump at a chance to shape themselves into the kind of person or being that arouses them. And, of course, some people are simply seekers of new sensations, chasing the high of experiencing something they have never experienced before. Imagine if someone offered one such the ability to gain entirely new senses with which they can explore! You kinda don\u2019t have to, as one of the sample Elect is, in fact, basically this, though with only a light nod to fetish.<br /> <br /> However, the most intriguing ideas to me are twofold. The first involves corruption kink, which is almost a form of archetype play, only the archetype in question is simply \u201cworse\u201d (whatever that means in the role-play or for the individual in question). Parts of the description of the Autourgic in the book almost play into this, even sounding like something that might be said in the context of that kink, such as \u201cLook at what you are now. Look what you wanted.\u201d The other is someone in a Total Power Exchange relationship who is ordered to choose the Divergence. Arguably, that latter would count more as a Epimorph instead.<br /> <br /> The Epimorph (from \u1f10\u03c0\u1fd0\u0301 \u201con, upon, on top of\u201d and \u03bc\u03bf\u03c1\u03c6\u03ae \u201cshape, form, appearance, kind, type\u201d; presumably it was chosen in reference to a healing process wherein specialized cells revert to less specialized cells, which . . . is kinda creepy, to be honest) are those who Volunteered for the Divergence, even if they didn\u2019t pursue it for its own sake. In addition to the idea mentioned at the end of the previous paragraph, I had only a single immediate character come to mind: a Dominant or sadist who might be newer to the scene than their submissive or masochistic partner and thus feels inadequate, not quite able to give their partner what they want and need. In my experience, this feeling is both common and almost always wrong. However, I can fully see someone in the Chronicles of Darkness turning to the Divergence as a way to reshape themselves into what they think a \u201cproper\u201d or \u201ctrue\u201d Dominant or sadist should be. I used the other side of this (a submissive who felt like they weren\u2019t a good enough submissive) in <i>Sophia in Chains: The Safeword is Paradox</i>, actually.<br /> <br /> The Genotypal (we all know what that means, right?) or Born seem to lend themselves to the idea of a second-generation kinkster. Not someone \u201cborn into it\u201d (vomit) or a victim of certain things, but simply someone, like many of my friends, whose parents/parenting community happen to have also been involved in the community and who thus has more ability to contextualize their interests/identity and some readymade protectors when they enter the scene as an adult ~ and who (hopefully) also has some excellent practice drawing healthy boundaries! To be honest, that is the only character concept that suggested itself to me for the Genotypal ~ I suspect that they are the hardest to fit into this campaign concept . . . .</div> </div> </div> </div>Surprisingly enough, I had not even seen the connection between Autourgics and transformation content; one can certainly see the parallels there. Really, anything that involves atypical forms of the body could relate back to that topic, as you pointed out. The system of Variations and Scars that Deviant presents can actually mechanically represent body horror, even just the body in general.<br /> <br /> I must admit that I had not thought of Epimorphs in relation to the topic, largely due to the fact that they are associated with darker elements, but I think that they could pose some interesting stories. I think that the nature of consent is the thing that is really dealt with when it comes to Epimorphs. They bring to mind someone who is not fully given to a given pursuit, but who becomes involved with it for other reasons. I think that Epimorphs would certainly be more obviously victimized than Autourgics, and you could thus have plotlines that related to that, but they would still be not quite as bad off as those who were plainly forced to undergo the Divergence.<br /> <br /> I had actually thought about Genotypals and their relation to the topic at hand, funnily enough, although I think that my thoughts regarding them differed from yours. I had not actually thought of the parallels between Genotypals and those who have access to information regarding fetishism through their community. I can certainly see the connection there, though. Personally, I had thought that Genotypals sort of represented the classic question of where our predispositions come from. This really extends beyond just fetishism and encompasses all mental traits, as it is actually really difficult to form a coherent theory for how attributes are acquired, whether they be biological, learned, spiritual, or come from some other area. It does seem as though Deviant touches on that area slightly, for the books talks about how the nature of the soul and its incarnation is largely a mystery. The explicit reference to pre-existence was notable given how philosophers have specifically noted the relevance of that very topic to the psychology of personality in the past. Fetishism itself is a very odd thing, and the exact way in which it manifests in human beings is still unknown despite over a century of study. Can certain behaviors and personality traits be derived from genetics? Can fetishes be learned? If so, why do only some people develop them? And then there are other matters to consider, like innate attributes that are not physical in nature. Such models can even be combined with one another. An example would be that of the model in which personality attributes are uncreated, and they incarnate in human beings through the mechanisms of nature, which can include things like conditioning. I think that Genotypals have a lot of good storytelling hooks in that area.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28352-verlan","name":"Verlan","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"118"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}